WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.589 --> 00:00:02.739
(playful music)

00:00:02.739 --> 00:00:06.239
- The trick is trying to think like a cat.

00:00:07.164 --> 00:00:08.818
It's one thing to think,
"What would it be like

00:00:08.818 --> 00:00:09.826
if I were a cat?”

00:00:09.826 --> 00:00:12.040
It's another thing to
try to figure out, “Well,

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.130
what is it like for a cat to be a cat?”

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:16.297
(purring)

00:00:18.042 --> 00:00:19.722
I have different ways of creating

00:00:19.722 --> 00:00:21.879
(deep string music)

00:00:21.879 --> 00:00:23.546
purr kind of sounds.

00:00:24.892 --> 00:00:26.091
I'm David Teie,

00:00:26.091 --> 00:00:30.274
classical musician, mostly
by trade, play the cello

00:00:30.274 --> 00:00:33.282
in the National Symphony,
and I came up with the idea

00:00:33.282 --> 00:00:35.503
of writing music for cats.

00:00:35.503 --> 00:00:38.754
Here are my bird songs for study

00:00:38.754 --> 00:00:40.722
(birds chirping)

00:00:40.722 --> 00:00:42.093
and I slow them down.

00:00:42.093 --> 00:00:44.040
(birds chirping)

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:48.618
Cats are not very vocal and
not particularly social.

00:00:48.618 --> 00:00:51.895
The ultimate goal is to
be able to communicate

00:00:51.895 --> 00:00:54.647
in something like their language.

00:00:54.647 --> 00:00:56.698
- [Male Interviewer] What
other sounds are there

00:00:56.698 --> 00:00:59.756
that you know cats would enjoy?

00:00:59.756 --> 00:01:02.028
- Most of the brain development of cat

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:03.798
happens outside the womb.

00:01:03.798 --> 00:01:07.788
I thought the sound of suckling
is a reward-related sound

00:01:07.788 --> 00:01:11.915
that all cats will have heard
as their brains were forming,

00:01:11.915 --> 00:01:15.815
this swishing kind of
(mocks swishing) sound.

00:01:15.815 --> 00:01:17.415
Yeah, I would totally use that.

00:01:17.415 --> 00:01:20.355
Could I name that after your jacket?

00:01:20.355 --> 00:01:22.438
That's my Louis W. swish.

00:01:25.547 --> 00:01:29.656
Another example of a sound is the purring.

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:30.947
- [Male Interviewer]
When you play this music,

00:01:30.947 --> 00:01:33.795
how does a cat react exactly?

00:01:33.795 --> 00:01:35.955
- [David] There are things
like that they would

00:01:35.955 --> 00:01:39.827
rub up against the speakers
that shows, kind of, ownership,

00:01:39.827 --> 00:01:43.915
and but showing just
attention, and it tends to be

00:01:43.915 --> 00:01:47.315
more comforting to those cats who have had

00:01:47.315 --> 00:01:48.856
something of a hard life.

00:01:48.856 --> 00:01:53.606
The cats who need more comfort
are more comforted by it.

00:01:53.606 --> 00:01:55.256
- [Male Interviewer] Do you have a cat?

00:01:55.256 --> 00:01:56.725
- No, I actually don't.

00:01:56.725 --> 00:01:58.678
I'm allergic to cats.

00:01:58.678 --> 00:02:00.214
- [Male Interviewer]
You're allergic to cats?

00:02:00.214 --> 00:02:01.552
- [David] Yeah.

00:02:01.552 --> 00:02:04.885
(pleasant string music)

